# ── REGRESSION WITNESSES — NOT deployed rules, and deliberately not in prometheusrule.yaml ─────
# The pre-#332 exprs kept verbatim, plus one half-fix counterfactual, so agent-router-witness
# .promtool-test can assert the FAILURE that was fixed instead of merely asserting the fix. Same
# device as subscription.promtool-rules §regression-witness-pre-288 and github-exporter/
# loop-health.promtool-rules §regression-witness-pre-313, and for the same reason: it makes the
# contrast executed rather than claimed. It is also why a STALE hand copy of the shipped group
# cannot pass vacuously — with the old expr in both places the two alerts would agree, and every
# roll assertion in the witness test fails.
#
# If either shipped expr is ever revisited, these stay as they are: they are fossils of what must
# not come back, not copies of the live rules. Nothing regenerates this file.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE, unlike the two precedents that put the witness group at the
# bottom of the shipped rules file. promtool evaluates EVERY rule in EVERY loaded rule file at
# EVERY step of EVERY test in the file — not only the rules an assertion names (verified: an
# unasserted rule's evaluation error still failed the run). `OpenRouterKeyBudgetLowPre332` errors
# out — `found duplicate series for the match group {key="…"}; many-to-many matching not allowed`
# — the instant two series share a `key`, which is exactly the input the shipped fixture's
# §masking case needs. Co-locating them would have meant dropping that case. So: shipped
# behaviour in agent-router.promtool-test, the contrast here, and every case in THIS file keeps
# ≥6m between pod generations so the 5m PromQL lookback expires and the witness never sees two.
groups:
  - name: regression-witness-pre-332
    rules:
      # RouterRotationStale as it stood before #332. Both mechanisms are live in it: `pod`/
      # `instance` ride the alert (identity churn) and there is no range to bridge the hole.
      - alert: RouterRotationStalePre332
        expr: router_rotation_age_seconds{source="openrouter-daily-rankings"} > 259200
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: OpenRouter rankings rotation feed is stale (>3d)

      # THE TWO HALF-FIXES for RouterRotationStale, so #288's "either half alone still resets the
      # window" is executed here rather than inherited as a claim. Identity-only survives the SHORT
      # roll in the fixture and would not in production (no staleness marker here — see the test
      # file's §W1 note); range-only fails at any hole size, because a new pod is still a new alert
      # instance with its own clock.
      - alert: RouterRotationStaleIdentityOnly
        expr: max by (source) (router_rotation_age_seconds{source="openrouter-daily-rankings"}) > 259200
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning

      - alert: RouterRotationStaleRangeOnly
        expr: max_over_time(router_rotation_age_seconds{source="openrouter-daily-rankings"}[10m]) > 259200
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning

      # OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow as it stood before #332. Note what it does NOT suffer from: a
      # one-to-one `/ on (key)` already reduced the result to `{key}`, so identity churn was never
      # this alert's problem. The hole is — and a hole on EITHER side empties the whole match.
      - alert: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLowPre332
        expr: >-
          router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd / on(key) router_openrouter_key_limit_usd < 0.10
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: none

      # THE HALF-FIX, the one #288 warned about: identity handled, hole not. For this alert
      # `max by (key)` on its own is a no-op on the result labels (see above), so it is a pure
      # demonstration that the range is the half that matters here — it goes silent across the
      # same roll the shipped expr rides through.
      #
      # The MIRROR half-fix — range without `max by (key)` — is not here because it cannot be:
      # `max_over_time` makes both pod generations resolvable at one instant, two series share a
      # `key`, and `on (key)` raises a many-to-one ERROR rather than returning nothing. promtool
      # has no expect-error assertion, so that half is verified by hand (promtool 3.13.1) and
      # recorded in the manifest comment; the shipped fixture asserts the positive instead.
      - alert: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLowIdentityOnly
        expr: >-
          max by (key) (router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd)
          / on (key)
          max by (key) (router_openrouter_key_limit_usd)
          < 0.10
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: none
